      <h2 id="buying-guide">Buying Guide: How to Pick the Right Platform</h2>
      <p><strong>Pick Shopify if</strong> you want the fastest launch, app coverage for every use case, a predictable all-in-one price, and a clear enterprise path to Shopify Plus. Most merchants launching in 2026 should start on Shopify — it has the lowest failure rate from misconfigured hosting or plugin conflicts.</p>
      <p><strong>Pick WooCommerce if</strong> you already run WordPress, content marketing and SEO are your primary growth channel, you need complete technical control, or you have access to reliable WordPress developers who can maintain the stack. WooCommerce is also common for agencies that build stores for clients.</p>
      <p><strong>Pick BigCommerce if</strong> you are above $200k/year and Shopify's transaction fees and app costs are stacking up, you need B2B or multi-storefront natively, or you plan to move headless and want a SaaS platform with strong APIs. Many Shopify Plus alternatives evaluate BigCommerce Enterprise first.</p>
      <p><strong>Pick Squarespace if</strong> you sell under 200 SKUs, visual presentation is your primary differentiator, and you do not need advanced fulfillment, marketplace selling, or multi-currency B2B features. Creators and lifestyle brands tend to stay on Squarespace for years.</p>
      <p>Common mistakes to avoid: do not pick WooCommerce without a WordPress developer or managed host on retainer; do not pick Squarespace if you plan to scale past $2M/year without a migration budget; and do not pick Shopify Basic if your average order value is below $30 and you use an external payment gateway — the transaction fee math gets ugly fast.</p>
      <p>Learn more about our <a href="/how-we-test/">testing methodology</a> and <a href="/editorial-policy/">editorial policy</a>.</p>

      <h2 id="faq">Frequently Asked Questions</h2>
      <div class="faq">
        <details>
          <summary>Which ecommerce platform is best for beginners?</summary>
          <p>Shopify is the best ecommerce platform for beginners in 2026. Its out-of-the-box hosted solution means you do not need to manage hosting, security patches, or updates. The onboarding wizard walks you through adding your first product, connecting a payment gateway, and choosing a theme in under an hour. If you already have a WordPress site, WooCommerce is a strong beginner-friendly alternative because it uses WordPress plugins and themes you may already know.</p>
        </details>
        <details>
          <summary>Is Shopify cheaper than WooCommerce?</summary>
          <p>Shopify Basic starts at $39 per month and includes hosting, SSL, and the checkout. WooCommerce is free as a plugin, but you pay separately for hosting ($5–$30/mo), a premium theme ($50–$200 one-time), payment gateway fees, and often maintenance or developer time. For stores under $50k/year, Shopify's total cost is usually lower and more predictable. For stores above $200k/year, WooCommerce can be cheaper if you already have a stable WordPress stack.</p>
        </details>
        <details>
          <summary>Which platform has the best SEO?</summary>
          <p>WooCommerce (on WordPress) has the strongest SEO ceiling because you control every URL, meta tag, page speed optimization, and content strategy with plugins like Yoast SEO and Rank Math. Shopify is strong for ecommerce SEO out of the box — clean URLs, automatic sitemaps, structured data, and fast CDN — but you have less control over technical SEO details. BigCommerce also has strong built-in SEO. Squarespace is the weakest of the four for granular SEO control.</p>
        </details>
        <details>
          <summary>Which platform scales best for high-volume stores?</summary>
          <p>BigCommerce scales best for high-volume stores because its Enterprise plan handles tens of millions in annual revenue without extra transaction fees or the need to bolt on a headless architecture. Shopify Plus is the market leader for enterprise DTC brands and scales reliably, but it uses Shopify Pay or requires negotiation on custom enterprise pricing. WooCommerce scales only if you invest in managed WordPress hosting, page caching, and a custom checkout — most stores above $2M/year migrate away from WooCommerce to Shopify Plus or BigCommerce.</p>
        </details>
      </div>

      <AffiliateDisclosure />
